\n\nHmm: display = [0,9,9,9,0,0] (6 digits). Array 0x07DE..0x07E2 = [0,9,9,9,0] (5 values). Plus display's last digit 0. So display = [0x07DE, 0x07DF, 0x07E0, 0x07E1, 0x07E2, fixed 0]. So the array provides the top 5 digits and the ones place is fixed 0, and the 6th displayed digit (10s) is 0x07E2.\n\nWait: display[0]=0x07DE, display[1]=0x07DF, display[2]=0x07E0, display[3]=0x07E1, display[4]=0x07E2, display[5]=0 (fixed).\n= [0,9,9,9,0,0]. Yes! So the score is 5 stored digits (0x07DE-0x07E2) + ones fixed 0 = a 6-digit score where the last digit is always 0. That makes sense: SMB scores are always multiples of 10.\n\nHmm, but then 0x07DD is... unused? Or it's a leading extra byte. But the mapping note said score is 0x07DD-0x07E2. Hmm.\n\nHold on — maybe the score is 6 stored digits at 0x07DE-0x07E3? Let me check 0x07E3. In the latest read, I wrote 0x07E3? I didn't. Let me check the cbefore→cafter diff or read 0x07E3.\n\nActually, let me look at the earlier result: I wrote 0x07DD..0x07E2 = [0,0,1,2,3,4], and the display showed 012540 with RAM [0,0,1,2,5,4]. Display = array[1..5]+0 = [0,1,2,5,4,0] = 012540. Yes matches!\n\nSo the display reads 0x07DE..0x07E2 (5 bytes) + fixed 0. So which bytes are the score? 0x07DE-0x07E2. And 0x07DD is not displayed.\n\nHmm, but wait — maybe 0x07DD is displayed as the leading digit only when the score is ≥ 1,000,000 (7 digits)? SMB max score is 999999 (6 digits). Hmm.\n\nActually, I bet the array is 0x07DD-0x07E2 = 6 bytes but the display logic maps them shifted, OR the display starts at col 4 and col 3 is a separate leading \"0\". Let me re-check: the mapping note said \"Nametable 0 row 3 cols 4-8 and 12-14 hold tile 0\". cols 4-8 = 5 tiles. Hmm, and the score is 6 digits? The note says \"the score/coin digits\". \n\nHmm, but I observed score digits at cols 3-8 (6 tiles). Let me double check by reading a wider region and seeing the layout: col 3 is '0' always? In both observations, col 3 = 0. And the display \"099900\" had col3=0. If