ually the coin display is 2 digits at cols 13-14 (tile 0 and 9 → 09). Coin=9 (0x07EE=9). So the coin display uses 2 digits. Let me test the coin extent too: write 0x07ED=1.., 0x07EE=2, 0x07EF=3, cause redraw, see which appear. Actually the coin is a 2-digit counter; likely stored as 1 byte BCD (0x07EE) and displayed as tens/ones. 9 shows as \"09\". If it's a single byte BCD, 0x07EE=9 → 09. And 0x07ED/0x07EF irrelevant.\n\nHmm, but wait: SMB coins go up to 99 then wrap? Actually coins count up to 99. Stored as 1 byte BCD? 0x07EE=9 → display 09. Let me test 0x07EE = 0x42? No, BCD would show 42. Actually if 0x07EE is a single byte binary, 9→09, 10→10, 42→42. If BCD, 0x42 → 42.\n\nHmm, hold on. 0x07EE=1 displayed x01, 0x07EE=8 → x08, 0x07EE=9 → x09. Consistent with either binary or BCD for these values. Let me test with a value like 42 (0x2A) or 12: write 0x07EE=12 (binary) → if binary, display 12; if BCD, 0x0C=12 decimal but the digit routine might show \"0C\"? Unlikely. Let me just test 0x07EE=12 and see if it shows 12. If it shows 12, binary. If weird, BCD.\n\nActually, SMB coin count max is 99. Standard SMB stores coins as a single byte binary at $07ED and displays via division. Hmm. Let me just test.\n\nFor the score, the display is digit-per-byte (I wrote 9 and it showed 9), so the score is a digit array (unpacked BCD, one digit per byte). Good.\n\nSo score = 5 or 6 bytes of one digit each at 0x07DD/0x07DE..0x07E2.\n\nLet me do the definitive extent experiment now. Write 0x07DC..0x07E3 = [1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8] and cause a redraw.\n\nLet me write and run.\n\nActually, I realize the current card screen is exactly the moment before the HUD redraws. Let me write now and run ~200 frames.\n\nLet me do it."}
