m the binary count, then writing 0x07EE=7 and collecting (7→8) would set tens = 8/10 = 0. 0x07ED was 0, stays 0. Consistent. And writing 0x07EE=0x10=16 and displaying would compute tens=1, ones=6, showing \"16\". But we saw \"9\" + tile 16 (raw). So the display does NOT compute; it copies. Therefore 0x07ED is a stored digit (or a display byte maintained separately). \n\nHmm, but if 0x07ED is a display byte maintained separately from a binary counter at 0x07EE, then writing 0x07EE=0x10 wouldn't update 0x07ED, so the display would show the stale 0x07ED (which I wrote as 9). That matches what we saw! And the display would copy 0x07EE raw (16) as the ones tile. So this scenario also matches: binary counter at 0x07EE, display tens at 0x07ED (updated by the coin routine).\n\nTo distinguish, I need to collect a coin with 0x07EE=16 or similar and see the resulting display. If the game does decimal increment on digit bytes, 0x07EE=16 (invalid) would... hmm.\n\nBetter distinguishing test: Set 0x07EE=9, 0x07ED=0, collect a coin.\n- If decimal digit counter: ones overflows → 0, tens → 1; display \"10\".\n- If binary counter at 0x07EE with computed tens: 9+1=10 (0x0A); the routine would compute tens=1, ones=0 → display \"10\". Same result!\n\nHmm, both give \"10\". Not distinguishing.\n\nTry: set 0x07EE=0x10 (16), 0x07ED=0, collect a coin.\n- Decimal digit counter: the routine reads ones=16, increments → 17, then maybe checks >9 → sets to 10? Depends. Could produce weird.\n- Binary counter: 16+1=17; tens=1, ones=7 → display \"17\".\n\nHmm.\n\nActually, for the purposes of this task, I think the natural answer is: the coin count is at 0x07ED (tens) and 0x07EE (ones)? Or the coin count is at 0x07EE? Let me think about what a \"RAM map\" would list. Standard SMB RAM map: \"$07ED - Coins\". Hmm, I'm fairly sure the standard map has the coin count at 0x07ED as ONE byte binary. But our experiment shows the display uses 0x07ED and 0x07EE as two digits.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, maybe I have the display position mapping wrong. Let me re-check: nametable cols 13,14 = tiles 9,16. And I wrote 0x07ED=9, 0x07EE=16. So col13←0x07ED, col14←0x07EE. But which is the tens and which the ones? The coin display is \"x\" then two digits. col13 is the first digit (tens), col14 the second (ones). So 0x07ED=tens, 0x07EE=ones.\n\nHmm OK. But actually, wait: is it possible that the coin count is at 0x07ED as a single binary byte, and 0x07EE is the ONES DIGIT display (updated by the routine), and 0x07ED happens to be both the count AND the tens display because for counts 0-9 the tens digit equals... no, for count 0-9, tens should be 0, but 0x07ED was 0 after collecting 1 coin (count=1, tens=0). Consistent. For count=10, 0x07ED would be 1. And the display tens=1. So 0x07ED could be the binary count that ALSO serves as the tens digit for counts 0-99? No, binary count 5 ≠ tens digit 0.\n\nHmm, for count=5, binary count=5, but tens digit should be 0. If 0x07ED=5, the display would show \"5\" + ones digit \"5\" = \"55\". Wrong. So 0x07ED is not the binary count.\n\nTherefore 0x07ED is the tens digit (0-9) and 0x07EE is the ones digit (0-9). The coin count = 10*0x07ED + 0x07EE. \n\nHmm, but then how does the game know the count to detect 100 coins (1-up)? It would check tens==9 && ones==9? Hmm.\n\nActually, you know what, let me reconsider: maybe the coin count is a single byte at 0x07EE stored in BCD! For BCD, 0x07EE=0x09 = 9, display \"09\" (tens = high nibble = 0, ones = low nibble = 9). And 0x07ED is a separate display byte? Hmm. But then writing 0x07EE=0x10 (BCD 10) → display \"10\" (tens=1, ones=0). But we saw col13=9 (from my write to 0x07ED) and col14=16. If the display computed from BCD 0x07EE=0x10, it would show \"10\", not \"9\"+tile16. So no.\n\nSo the display copies 0x07ED and 0x07EE directly as the two digit tiles. Hence they are two digit bytes.\n\nHmm, but that's weird because then 0x07ED is the \"tens of coins\" and 0x07EE the \"ones of coins\". Together they're the coin count.\n\nHmm, actually, maybe I should reconsider: perhaps the display routine reads a SINGLE byte and separates nibbles, adding a base tile, and writes two tiles. With the byte at 0x07EE: low nibble... no, we saw col13 from 0x07ED.\n\nOK, I'm confident: coin count occupies two digit bytes at 0x07ED (tens) and 0x07EE (ones), and the display copies them raw. Collecting a coin increments the ones (0x07EE) with carry to the tens (0x07ED).\n\nHmm, but hold on, let me reconsider whether the coin increment carries. Earlier I pre-wrote 0x07EE=7 and collected → 8. I never tested 9→carry. Let me do one more test to confirm the carry and thus the encoding. This will also strengthen the proof.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the task's likely expected answer. The task says \"the coin count\". The standard SMB RAM address for coins... Let me think: many SMB RAM maps list:\n- 0x07DD-0x07E2: Score\n- 0x07ED: Coins? \nHmm. But our data says 0x07EE is the ones digit.\n\nHmm, wait — maybe I have an off-by-one in the address base! Let me double-check the address mapping with an independent test. I wrote to address 2029 and read back 2029 → 9, and the display's first coin digit became 9. If the address mapping were off by one, then writing 2029 would affect 0x07ED and read back 2029=9 (consistent) — no, if off by one, read 2029 would show the value at 2030. Ugh, let me verify the read/write address consistency differently.\n\nActually, read_memory address 2029 returned [9] after writing 9 to 2029. And the display col13 became 9. And get_tile_at_pixel etc. are consistent. Also the timer: I wrote 2040=[1,2,3] and read 2040=[1,0,2] and the display showed 119/884/551. And 0x07F8=2040. If the true address were 2040, fine.\n\nLet me just double check the timer address is 0x07F8 and not 0x07F7. The timer display showed 551 when I wrote 555 to 2040. So 2040=tens? Actually 555 → displayed 551: hundreds=5, tens=5, ones=1. Bytes at 2040,2041,2042 = [5,5,1]. So 2040=hundreds, 2041=tens, 2042=ones. And 2040=0x07F8. So the mapping is correct: address 2040 ↔ 0x07F8.\n\nSo 2029=0x07ED, 2030=0x07EE. Confirmed.\n\nHmm OK. So this World version stores the coin count as two decimal digit bytes at 0x07ED-0x07EE. Hmm, but wait, maybe the coin count is actually a single byte at 0","truncated":true}
